.TH CSTR_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cstr_declare_scoped \- declare a cstr variable with automatic RAII destruction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>

cstr_declare_scoped(s);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is like cstr_declare(), but it also attaches a cleanup
attribute. The program therefore destroys the string automatically when the
variable leaves its scope. That destruction does exactly what
cstring_destroy() does: it frees the internal buffer and the container, and
it sets the variable to NULL. The macro initialises the variable to NULL.
You can therefore leave the variable unassigned for the whole scope, and
the cleanup then does nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
s   Name of the variable to declare
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This macro needs GCC or Clang, because it uses __attribute__((cleanup))
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
{
  cstr_declare_scoped(s);
  cstr_init(s, "auto-freed");
  /* the program destroys s automatically at the end of this block */
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cstr_declare (3),
.BR cstr_construct_scoped (3),
.BR cstring_destroy (3)
